= Heartland v. Commonwealth

The time has finally come for us, along with the <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>, and the state governments of Avriza and Barrowdale, to argue for the unconstitutionality of the Heartland <i>Commonwealth intervention</i>. However, the leader of the state court, and president of the supreme court, is Erwin Burnell, a known <span style="color: #808080;">reactionary</span> and former <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> member before taking his job. 

The main conditions under examination are whether the Heartland government failed to fulfill its constitutional obligations in maintaining public order and security, and whether the federal government had the constitutional right to intervene and appoint a Commonwealth Commissioner to take over governance in Heartland indefinitely.

- @papen
- @papen_2

@papen
title: We await the final verdict.
view-if: (judicial_reform < 4 and not altona_good) or (judicial_reform < 2 and altona_good)
on-arrival: pro_republic -= 3

The State Court has ruled that the Heartland government did not fail in its duties, meaning the use of force against a state refusing to follow the constitution was unlawful and the application of the <i>Commonwealth intervention</i> was unjustified. However, the court did affirm that the government could still appoint a Commonwealth Commissioner and temporarily assume control of Heartland due to a perceived threat to public safety and order.

Additionally, the court declared that the dismissal of the Heartland government was illegal and that Heartland’s representation in the Commonwealth Council must be restored. While acknowledging that the Heartland government remains legally intact, the judicial system’s <span style="color: #808080;">reactionary</span> nature meant that no practical change was enforced. The court’s ruling did not explicitly demand the reinstatement of the Heartland government, and the [+ chancellor +] government has ignored the decision. Holding the government accountable for this violation would require accusing President DuFour of breaching the law by signing the decree—an action the conservative judges find politically unacceptable.

@papen_2
title: We await the final verdict.
view-if: (judicial_reform >= 4 and not altona_good) or (judicial_reform >= 2 and altona_good)
on-arrival: heartland_returned = 1; heartland_legal_returned = 1; heartland_leader = older_heartland_leader; in_weimar_coalition_heartland = 1 if pre_in_weimar_coalition_heartland; in_grand_coalition_heartland = 1 if pre_in_grand_coalition_heartland; in_minority_weimar_coalition_heartland = 1 if in_weimar_coalition_heartland and weimar_coalition_heartland < 50; in_minority_grand_coalition_heartland = 1 if in_grand_coalition_heartland and grand_coalition_heartland < 50; pro_republic += 8; commons_marcher += 5; burgher_marcher += 5; disfavored_marcher += 3; burgher_ddp += 3; labor_dissent -= 15; reformist_dissent -= 15; left_dissent -= 15; heartland_police_loyalty += 0.3; banner_strength *= 1.1; banner_militancy *= 1.1; papen_strength -= 1; dufour_papen_r -= 1

The State Court has ruled that the Heartland government did not fail in its duties, declaring that the use of force against a state obeying the constitution was unlawful, and that the application of the <i>Commonwealth intervention</i> was unjustified. The court has ordered the federal government to relinquish control over Heartland's ministries and to restore its representation in the Commonwealth Council.

Goodryke complies with the ruling and reinstates the Heartland government under [+ heartland_leader +]. Unfortunately, they remain powerless against DuFour and Goodryke, who signed the decree in the first place.
